    {
      "paperId": "/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-60113-7_9",
      "doi": "10.1007/978-3-319-60113-7_9",
      "title": "Encouraging New Space Firms",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "John M. Logsdon"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2018,
      "abstract": "On the basis of work done as part of their studies at Harvard Business School and an initial investment by a Texas space enthusiast, in 1982 three young entrepreneurs created the Orbital Systems Corporation, later renamed Orbital Sciences Corporation. Targeted as the first product for that new firm was an upper stage to transfer payloads, especially communications satellites, from the payload bay of the space shuttle to their desired orbits. This upper stage was designated the “Transfer Orbit Stage.” Crucial to the viability of this project was NASA’s agreement not to use government funds to develop a similar capability. This agreement provided the basis for Orbital to move forward with its plans in a way that allowed it to survive the loss of the business base for the Transfer Orbit Stage, when the White House after the Challenger accident ruled that the shuttle would no longer carry commercial payloads. Orbital went on to develop a second product, an air-launched commercial rocket called Pegasus, which was used by both NASA and the Department of Defense. Pegasus was first launched in 1990 and in 2011 its development team was awarded the National Medal of Technology by President Bush. (John M. Logsdon)",
